Quoting  Dylan O'Donnell <psmith@spod-central.org>:
>"Kent Paul Dolan" <xanthian@well.com> writes:
>>This is too simple, of course, because the monster
>>mix is situational.
>I wonder if a reasonable approximation to the monster mix for this
>purpose could be found by analysing the vanquished lists from
>ascension dumps?

I don't think this exercise is meaningful at all. What you care about is
not weapon damage versus monsters-by-probability or weapon damage versus
monsters-by-typical-number-vaniquished. What you care about is the chance
that the monster kills you (directly or by contributing to an eventual
death) if you do it less damage; that's per-game and per-situation, and a
rough estimate (like the "let's worry about demons") in the current
spoiler is as good - better than - this approach.

This approach also neglects the "weapons golf bag"; if you were going to
carry a Foobane, you'd normally only whip it out to fight Foos. The real
question as to whether a Foobane is worth anything is a) how dangerous are
Foos and b) if Foos are dangerous at all, does a Foobane do more damage to
Foos than my current best non-Foobane weapon?
